  "slug": "untitled-topic-b83f",
  "summary": "Six preprints released 19–24 November extend microbiome, pathogen and metaproteomics analysis through interoperable, browser-based and reproducible tools.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is software that makes microbial sequence and proteomic analysis more accessible, interoperable and reproducible. VPHYL converts Emu and EPI2ME wf-16s nanopore 16S abundance tables into phyloseq and BIOM objects, while RADSuite pairs RADlib, a curated 16S rRNA gene copy database, with RADx to enable species-level identification. SNP2Cluster-Py clusters pathogen genomes by core SNP distance with epidemiological and temporal context, tested on Klebsiella and other data. Unipept's 2026 update brings multi-sample metaproteomics workflows into a web platform. Sparrowhawk uses WebAssembly for private in-browser microbial genomics, and a Fusion transpiler study tests whether SNHA network-inference code stays functionally identical across nine languages. The advances are at the tool level (workflow design, interoperability, privacy-preserving deployment, reproducibility) rather than new biological findings.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
